        <p class="eyebrow">ESSAY · AUG 21, 2026</p>
        <h1>On Keeping<br />Technical Notes</h1>
      </header>
      <p>In systems research, many decisions only look obvious while their context is still fresh. Months later, the conclusion remains, but the chain of evidence behind it has often disappeared.</p>
      <p>This blog is a place to preserve the reusable parts: how a performance problem was isolated, how an experiment ruled out a tempting explanation, and how a design survived the trade-offs among performance, area, and timing.</p>
      <h2>Write less, retain more</h2>
      <p>I do not plan to publish on a schedule or turn every attempt into a polished story. A note belongs here only when the problem and the evidence are clear enough to remain useful after the project has moved on.</p>
      <p>The goal is simple: years from now, these notes should still be accurate, honest, and useful.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
